WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.785 --> 00:00:04.528
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.528 --> 00:00:08.630
I am now introducing the median string
problem, and

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:11.690
to define this problem, we need to define
the notion

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:15.960
of distance not only between k-mers of the
same size,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:20.730
but also the distance between a k-mer and the
longer string.

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:25.150
So what is this distance between two
strings of different lengths.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:30.690
We simply start from a smaller string (a k-mer) and
compare it to the first k-mer

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:31.850
in the text.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:34.900
Distance in this case is seven, we move
further. distance

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.550
six, continue, continue, continue until
the end of the string.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:44.070
And then, return to the place where the
distance was minimal.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:50.580
So in this way, we will find a k-mer within
a longer string that is most close

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:55.350
to our k-mer of interest, and this is the
distance between a pattern and a string.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.770
What is the distance between a k-mer and a
set of strings.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:04.290
In this case, it's simply the sum of
distances

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.090
between a pattern and each string in the
set.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:15.030
For example, for a pattern AAA
and a set of strings shown on the slide,

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:21.190
we can compute distance with every string
and simply sum up all the distances.

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:21.970
The result

00:01:21.970 --> 00:01:27.260
is five.
And median string for the set of strings

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:32.620
Dna is a k-mer pattern minimizing distance
between pattern

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:37.910
and DNA over all possible k-mers.

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:41.830
And our median string problem will be
based on the

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:47.510
problem we formulated before, which is yet
another equivalent motif finding problem.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:50.940
If you remember this problem, then it
looks like

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.550
even more complicated than the original
motif finding problem,

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:57.105
because in this problem, we need to find 

00:01:57.105 --> 00:02:02.200
a k-mer and a set of motifs satisfying
certain properties.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.090
But in fact, the only thing we need to
find

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.930
is a k-mer pattern as shown in this median
string problem.

00:02:10.070 --> 00:02:12.774
In this case, we search for a k-mer

00:02:12.774 --> 00:02:17.410
pattern minimizing distance between this
pattern and the

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:22.290
set of strings Dna (among all possible k-mers).

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:26.440
Now, there is a very simple algorithm for
solving this problem.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:32.060
We simply need to try all possible k-mers
and see whether the

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:38.260
distance between the pattern and DNA is minimal

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:40.070
among all possible k-mer.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:45.659
The running time of this algorithm is 4 to
the power k times n*t*k, where 

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:47.470
we analyze 

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:54.010
t sequences of length n.
So what we have just accomplished?

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:57.300
We started from motif finding problem, and
the brute

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.450
force algorithm for solving this problem
was incredibly slow.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.520
We've turned it into yet another equivalent
motif finding problem,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:13.060
and through this problem, we were able to
switch to the median string problem

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.870
with this very different run time.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:19.040
In fact, it's still an exponential
algorithm

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:23.760
but for practical applications (where k
is usually less than 15)

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:29.070
we still can run this algorithm. So
dramatic improvement on the Motif Finding

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:35.690
Problem that we started from.
So sometimes, change of perspective helps.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:37.990
And to move further,

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:40.990
we will notice that although the Median String

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:44.210
Problem is much faster than the Motif Finding
Problem,

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:46.652
it's still slow if we search for

00:03:46.652 --> 00:03:47.318
long motifs

00:03:47.318 --> 00:03:52.270
and little bit later we will figure
out how to deal with such motifs.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:58.920
Our last topic in this segment is Greedy
Motif Search.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:03.585
We'll now talk about a greedy algorithm,
for solving the Motif Finding Problem.

00:04:03.585 --> 00:04:06.920
Given a set of motifs, 

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:10.030
we have already learned how to construct the
consensus string.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:12.400
Now let's construct the count matrix 

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:18.490
where in every column we simply
have counts for all nucleotides.

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:22.022
And also. this count matrix can be
transformed into the so-called

00:04:22.022 --> 00:04:28.426
profile matrix where we have
frequencies of nucleotides in every column.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.030
Now, these frequencies of nucleotides in
every column

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:37.720
can be viewed as a four-sided, biased
dice,

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:43.940
representing probabilities of ending up
on a given face of a dice.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:49.460
And we can view it as
a probability distribution.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:55.080
And if it is a die, let's ask a question:
"What is the probability that tossing

00:04:55.080 --> 00:05:02.150
such a die based on the motifs will generate
a given string of DNA?"

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:03.260
For example,

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:07.260
what will be the probability for
generating a string shown at this light?

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:12.800
So given the following profile, we ask,
what is

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.990
the probability of generating the
consensus string, AAACCT?

00:05:19.125 --> 00:05:23.000
And this probability will be simply
computed by

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.720
multiplication of corresponding elements
in the profile matrix.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:28.350
If you take

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:31.700
another string, we will get a different
probability.

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:37.900
And you have already noticed that the
closer the string is to the consensus

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:44.110
string of the profile, the higher is the
probability of generating this string.

00:05:45.670 --> 00:05:51.560
And now, we define the notion of Profile-most
probable k-mer in a sequence.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:53.640
This is a k-mer with the

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:59.140
highest probability among all k-mers in
the string.

00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:02.774
And here's an example of how I can

00:06:02.774 --> 00:06:07.080
generate Profile-most probable k-mer in a
string.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:12.690
We start from the first k-mer in the string,
compute probability,

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:15.510
and record it in the last column of the
matrix.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:19.320
We continue until we fill all the matrix,
and

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:24.550
finally, select the largest probability,
which in this case, corresponds to the

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:29.820
red entry in the matrix.
And with this at hand,

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:34.880
we are now ready for GreedyMotifSearch,
which works in a very

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:39.810
simple way.
We start from the set of i-1 motifs

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:44.505
selected from the first i-1 sequences

00:06:44.505 --> 00:06:48.600
and show how to extend this
set

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.960
of motifs by the motif in the i-th sequence.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:58.430
What we do, we form profile of motif from
the first i-1 1 sequences

00:06:58.430 --> 00:07:04.060
and then select profile most probable
k-mer in the sequence number i.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:06.410
Afterwards, we iterate.

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:09.606
And that will the result in the greedy
algorithm for

00:07:09.606 --> 00:07:11.260
solving the problem.

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:16.180
And we will see later whether it works or
doesn't work for our goals.

